  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>A small sandy cove with clear, shaded water, Graniers beach lies at the foot of the south slope of Saint-Tropez's Citadel, past the marine cemetery. It is only 94 metres long and bounded by rocks to the east and west; according to the tourist office, it has kept a wild, natural feel. A marine path runs nearby.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>As on all the town's beaches, municipal order no. 1828/2026 of 6 July 2026 allows dogs on the town's beaches from 6am to 9.30am and 6.30pm to 10pm, from 16 July to 15 September 2026; outside those hours they are not allowed. Dogs must stay under their owner's constant responsibility and be kept on a leash when approaching other users and whenever circumstances require; the order does not apply to walkers with dogs on a leash on the coastal path. It only covers that period: for the rest of the year, we found no published rule, so rely on the signage on site. The beach's listing on the tourist office's site says "dogs are not allowed" with no mention of hours — probably not updated since the order, but rely on the signage on site on the day of your visit.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the beach is small (94 m); in summer, the allowed windows for your dog are 6am-9.30am and 6.30pm-10pm.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The little <a href="/en/fiche-plages-centre-ville-saint-tropez">Ponche and Fontanette beaches</a> lie at the foot of the old village; <a href="/en/fiche-plage-canebiers-saint-tropez">Canebiers beach</a> is further east.</p>
    </div>

